    {
      "id": "PR_kwDOL-xLQ875OXgV",
      "title": "replica-healthcheck: switch to StaticJsonRpcProvider to investigate memory leak",
      "author": "sjonpaulbrown",
      "number": 694,
      "body": "## Summary\n- Swap `validators.provider` → `validators.staticJsonRpcProvider` for both `referenceRpcProvider` and `targetRpcProvider` in `HealthcheckService`.\n- Fix a latent null-deref in the `getBlock(minBlock)` null-check branches (was reading `.number` off the null value).\n\n## Context\n`replica-healthcheck` pods show a perfectly linear memory climb of ~5 MB/day (~85 bytes per 5s loop iteration), resetting only on restart, consistent across every replica over a 90d window. The shape rules out GC pressure and metric-cardinality growth — it points to a small object retained per RPC call.\n\nThe service itself is nearly stateless: two providers, a fixed set of label-less Prometheus metrics, no event subscriptions, no caches. The most plausible source is the non-`Static` `JsonRpcProvider` returned by `validators.provider`, which chains a `detectNetwork()` promise on every `send()`. `StaticJsonRpcProvider` skips that path.\n\n## Intended use\nDeploy to a single canary replica alongside unchanged replicas and observe RSS over 24–48h against the existing 90d graph. If the canary's line flattens while the others keep climbing, root cause is confirmed and we roll out fleet-wide. If it keeps climbing at the same rate, we widen the investigation (heap snapshot diff on a long-lived pod).\n\n## Test plan\n- [ ] CI green\n- [ ] Deploy to one replica; confirm `/metrics` and `/healthz` still respond and divergence detection still fires on a known-diverged pair\n- [ ] Watch `container_memory_working_set_bytes{pod=~\"replica-healthcheck-.*\"}` for the canary vs fleet over 24–48h\n- [ ] Decision: roll out fleet-wide OR proceed to heap snapshot investigation\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
      "repository": "ethereum-optimism/infra",
      "createdAt": "2026-07-31T22:27:44Z",
      "mergedAt": "2026-08-03T20:56:03Z",
